<P><A id=3DHistory name=3DHistory></A></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A title=3D"Edit section: History"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D1">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>History</SPAN></H2>
<P>The middle 19th century through the early 20th century saw the =
developments=20
that led to today's modern forklifts. The <A title=3D"Pennsylvania =
Railroad"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pennsylvania_Railroad">Pennsylvania =

Railroad</A> in 1906 introduced battery powered platform trucks for =
moving=20
luggage at their Altoona, Pennsylvania train station. <A title=3D"World =
War I"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I">World War I</A> saw =
the=20
development of different types of material handling equipment in the =
United=20
Kingdom by Ransomes, Sims and Jeffries of Ipswich. This was in part due =
to the=20
labor shortages caused by the war. In 1917 <A=20
title=3D"Clark Material Handling Company"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clark_Material_Handling_Company">Cla=
rk</A> in=20
the United States began developing and using powered tractor and powered =
lift=20
tractors in their factories. In 1919 the Towmotor Company and Yale &amp; =
Towne=20
Manufacturing in 1920 entered the lift truck market in the United =
States.<SUP=20
class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-1><A title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-1">[2]</A></SUP><=
/P>
<P>Continuing development and expanded use of the forklift continued =
through the=20
1920s and 1930s. <A title=3D"World War II"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II">World War II</A>, =
like <A=20
title=3D"World War I" =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I">World War=20
I</A> before, spurred the use of forklift trucks in the war effort. =
Following=20
the war, more efficient methods for storing products in warehouses were =
being=20
implemented. Warehouses needed more maneuverable forklift trucks that =
could=20
reach greater heights. New forklift models were made that filled this =
need.<SUP=20
class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-2><A title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-2">[3]</A></SUP><=
/P>
<P><A id=3DDesign_types name=3DDesign_types></A></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A title=3D"Edit section: Design types"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D2">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Design types</SPAN></H2>
<DIV class=3D"thumb tright">
<DIV class=3Dthumbinner style=3D"WIDTH: 302px"><A class=3Dimage=20
title=3D"Forklift classes.JPG"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Forklift_classes.JPG"><IMG=20
class=3Dthumbimage height=3D404 alt=3D""=20
src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/5c/Forklift_class=
es.JPG/300px-Forklift_classes.JPG"=20
width=3D300 border=3D0></A>=20
<DIV class=3Dthumbcaption>
<DIV class=3Dmagnify><A class=3Dinternal title=3DEnlarge=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Forklift_classes.JPG"><IMG =
height=3D11=20
alt=3D"" =
src=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify-clip.png" =

width=3D15></A></DIV></DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>The following is a list of the more common lift truck types. It is =
arranged=20
from the smallest type of lift to largest:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><A title=3D"Pallet jack"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pallet_jack">Hand pallet =
truck</A>=20
  <LI>Walkie low lift truck (powered pallet truck, usually electrically =
powered)=20

  <LI>Rider low lift truck=20
  <LI>Towing tractor=20
  <LI>Walkie stacker=20
  <LI>Rider stacker=20
  <LI>Reach truck (small forklift, designed for small aisles, usually=20
  electrically powered)=20
  <LI>Electric counterbalanced truck=20
  <LI>IC counterbalanced truck=20
  <LI>Sideloader=20
  <LI><A title=3D"Telescopic handler"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telescopic_handler">Telescopic =
handler</A>=20
  <LI>Slip Sheet machine=20
  <LI>Walkie Order Picking truck=20
  <LI>Rider Order Picking truck (commonly called an "Order Picker"; like =
a small=20
  forklift, except the operator rides up to the load and transfers it =
article by=20
  article)=20
  <LI>Articulated Very Narrow Aisle Counterbalanced trucks (commonly =
called=20
  "Flexi Truck")=20
  <LI>Guided Very Narrow Aisle truck - 'Man Down' (a type of reach truck =

  designed for aisles less than five feet wide) and 'Man Riser' =
Combination=20
  pickcle Picker/ Stacker truck=20
  <LI>Truck Mounted Forklift / Sod Loader </LI></UL>
<P><A id=3DSpecialty_trucks name=3DSpecialty_trucks></A></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A title=3D"Edit section: Specialty =
trucks"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D3">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Specialty trucks</SPAN></H3>
<P>At the other end of the spectrum from the counterbalanced forklift =
trucks are=20
more 'high end' specialty trucks:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Articulated Counterbalance Trucks</B> </LI></UL>
<P>These are, unlike most other lift trucks, front wheel steer, and are =
a hybrid=20
VNA (Very Narrow Aisle) truck designed to be both able to offload =
trailers and=20
place the load in narrow aisle racking. Increasingly these trucks are =
able to=20
compete in terms of pallet storage density, lift heights and pallet =
throughput=20
with Guided Very Narrow Aisle trucks.</P>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Guided Very Narrow Aisle Trucks</B> </LI></UL>
<P>These are rail or wide guided and available with lift heights up to =
40' non=20
top-tied and 98' top-tied. Two forms are available; 'man-down' and =
'man-riser'=20
where the operator elevates with the load for increased visibility or =
for=20
multilevel 'break bulk' order picking. This type of truck, unlike =
Articulated=20
Narrow Aisle Trucks, requires a high standard of floor flatness.</P>
<UL>
  <LI><B>U.S. Military 10K-AT "Adverse Terrain"</B> </LI></UL>
<P><A id=3DCounterbalanced_forklift_components=20
name=3DCounterbalanced_forklift_components></A></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Counterbalanced forklift components"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D4">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Counterbalanced forklift =
components</SPAN></H2>
<DIV class=3D"thumb tright">
<DIV class=3Dthumbinner style=3D"WIDTH: 302px"><A class=3Dimage=20
title=3D"Image of an electric forklift with component descriptions"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Forklift_Truck.jpg"><IMG=20
class=3Dthumbimage height=3D225 alt=3D""=20
src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Forklift_=
Truck.jpg/300px-Forklift_Truck.jpg"=20
width=3D300 border=3D0></A>=20
<DIV class=3Dthumbcaption>
<DIV class=3Dmagnify><A class=3Dinternal title=3DEnlarge=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Forklift_Truck.jpg"><IMG =
height=3D11=20
alt=3D"" =
src=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify-clip.png" =

width=3D15></A></DIV>Image of an electric forklift with component=20
descriptions</DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>A typical counterbalanced forklift contains the following =
components:<SUP=20
class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-3><A title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-3">[4]</A></SUP><=
/P>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Truck Frame</B> - is the base of the machine to which the =
mast,=20
  axles, wheels, counterweight, overhead guard and power source are =
attached.=20
  The frame may have fuel and hydraulic fluid tanks constructed as part =
of the=20
  frame assembly. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The <A title=3DCounterweight=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Counterweight">Counterweight</A></B>=
 - is a=20
  heavy <A title=3D"Cast iron" =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cast_iron">cast=20
  iron</A> mass attached to the rear of the forklift truck frame. The =
purpose of=20
  the counterweight is to counterbalance the load being lifted. In an =
electric=20
  forklift the large lead-acid battery itself may serve as part of the=20
  counterweight. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Cab</B> - is the area that contains a seat for the operator =
along=20
  with the control <A title=3DPedal=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pedal">pedals</A>, <A=20
  title=3D"Steering wheel"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steering_wheel">steering =
wheel</A>, <A=20
  title=3DLever href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lever">levers</A>, =
<A=20
  title=3DSwitch =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Switch">switches</A> and a=20
  dashboard containing operator readouts. The cab area may be open air =
or=20
  enclosed, but it is covered by the cage-like overhead guard assembly. =
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Overhead Guard</B> - is a metal <A title=3DRoof=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roof">roof</A> supported by posts =
at each=20
  corner of the cab that helps protect the operator from any falling =
objects. On=20
  some forklifts, the overhead guard is part of the frame assembly =
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Power Source</B> - may consist of an <A=20
  title=3D"Internal combustion engine"=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internal_combustion_engine">internal=
=20
  combustion engine</A> that can be powered by <A=20
  title=3D"Liquefied petroleum gas"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liquefied_petroleum_gas">LP =
gas</A>, <A=20
  title=3D"Compressed natural gas"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compressed_natural_gas">CNG =
gas</A>, <A=20
  title=3DGasoline =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gasoline">gasoline</A> or <A=20
  title=3DDiesel href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diesel">diesel</A> =
fuel.=20
  Electric forklifts are powered by either a <A title=3D"Lead-acid =
battery"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lead-acid_battery">battery</A> or =
<A=20
  title=3D"Fuel cell" =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuel_cell">fuel cells</A>=20
  that provide power to electric motors. The motors may be either <A=20
  title=3D"Direct current"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Direct_current">DC</A> or <A=20
  title=3D"Alternating current"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alternating_current">AC</A> =
types. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Tilt Cylinders</B> - are hydraulic cylinders that are mounted =
to the=20
  truck frame and the mast. The tilt cylinders pivot the mast to assist =
in=20
  engaging a load. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Mast</B> - is the vertical assembly that does the work of =
raising=20
  and lowering the load. It is made up of interlocking rails that also =
provide=20
  lateral stability. The interlocking rails may either have rollers or =
bushings=20
  as guides. The mast is either <A class=3Dmw-redirect title=3DHydraulic =

  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hydraulic">hydraulically</A> =
operated by=20
  one or more hydraulic cylinders or it may be chain operated with a =
hydraulic=20
  motor providing motive power. It may be mounted to the front axle or =
the frame=20
  of the forklift. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Carriage</B> - is the component to which the forks or other =

  attachments mount. It is mounted into and moves up and down the mast =
rails by=20
  means of chains or by being directly attached to the hydraulic =
cylinder. Like=20
  the mast, the carriage may have either rollers or bushings to guide it =
in the=20
  interlocking mast rails. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>The Load Back Rest</B> - is a rack-like extension that is =
either bolted=20
  or welded to the carriage in order to prevent the load from shifting =
backward=20
  when the carriage is lifted to full height. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Attachments</B> - may consist of forks or tines that are the =
L-shaped=20
  members that engage the load. A variety of other types of material =
handling=20
  attachments are available. These include sideshifters, slipsheet =
attachments,=20
  carton clamps, multipurpose clamps, rotators, fork positioners, carpet =
poles,=20
  pole handlers, container handlers, roll clamps and many others. =
</LI></UL>
<P><A id=3DAttachments name=3DAttachments></A></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A title=3D"Edit section: Attachments"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D5">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Attachments</SPAN></H3>
<P>Below is a list of common forklift attachments:<SUP class=3Dreference =

id=3Dcite_ref-4><A title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-4">[5]</A></SUP><=
/P>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Sideshifter</B> - is a hydraulic attachment that allows the =
operator to=20
  move the tines (forks) and backrest laterally. This allows easier =
placement of=20
  a load without having to reposition the truck.<SUP class=3Dreference=20
  id=3Dcite_ref-5><A title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-5">[6]</A></SUP> =

</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Rotator</B> - To aid the handling of skids that may have become =

  excessively tilted and other specialty material handling needs some =
forklifts=20
  are fitted with an attachment that allows the tines to be rotated. =
This type=20
  of attachment may also be used for dumping containers for quick =
unloading.=20
  </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Fork Positioner</B> - is a hydraulic attachment that moves the =
tines=20
  (forks) together or apart. This removes the need for the operator to =
get out=20
  of the cab to manually adjust the tines for different sized loads. =
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Roll and Barrel Clamp Attachment</B> - A mechanical or =
hydraulic=20
  attachment that is used for handling barrels, kegs, or paper rolls. =
This type=20
  of attachment also usually have a rotate function so that a vertically =
stored=20
  paper roll can be inserted into the horizontal intake of a printing =
press.=20
  </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Pole Attachments</B> - In some locations, such as carpet =
warehouses, a=20
  long metal pole is used instead of forks to lift carpet rolls. Similar =

  devices, though much larger, are used to pick up metal coils. =
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Carton and Multipurpose Clamp Attachments</B> - are hydraulic=20
  attachments that allow the operator to open and close around a load, =
squeezing=20
  it to pick it up. Products like cartons, boxes and bales can be moved =
with=20
  this type attachment. With these attachments in use, the forklift =
truck is=20
  sometimes referred to as a clamp truck. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B><A title=3D"Slip Sheet"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slip_Sheet">Slip Sheet</A> =
Attachment (Push=20
  - Pull)</B> - is a hydraulic attachment that reaches forward, clamps =
onto a <A=20
  title=3D"Slip Sheet"=20
  href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slip_Sheet">slipsheet</A> and =
draws the=20
  slipsheet onto wide and thin metal forks for transport. The attachment =
will=20
  push the slip sheet and load off the forks for placement. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Drum Handler Attachment</B> - is a mechanical attachment that =
slides=20
  onto the tines (forks). It usually has a spring loaded jaw that grips =
the top=20
  lip edge of a drum for transport. Another type grabs around the drum =
in a=20
  manner similar to the roll or barrel attachments. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Man Basket</B> - a lift platform that slides onto the tines =
(forks);=20
  meant for hoisting workers. The man basket has railings and brackets =
for=20
  attaching safety harnesses. </LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Telescopic Forks</B> - are hydraulic attachments that allow the =

  operator to operate in warehouse design for "double-deep stacking", =
which=20
  means that two pallet shelves are placed behind each other without any =
aisle=20
  between them. </LI></UL>
<P><A id=3DForklift_control_and_capabilities=20
name=3DForklift_control_and_capabilities></A></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Forklift control and capabilities"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D6">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Forklift control and capabilities</SPAN></H2>
<DIV class=3D"thumb tright">
<DIV class=3Dthumbinner style=3D"WIDTH: 152px"><A class=3Dimage=20
title=3D"Load capacity chart"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Plaque-de-charge-Chariot.jpg">=
<IMG=20
class=3Dthumbimage height=3D88 alt=3D""=20
src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Plaque-de=
-charge-Chariot.jpg/150px-Plaque-de-charge-Chariot.jpg"=20
width=3D150 border=3D0></A>=20
<DIV class=3Dthumbcaption>
<DIV class=3Dmagnify><A class=3Dinternal title=3DEnlarge=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:Plaque-de-charge-Chariot.jpg">=
<IMG=20
height=3D11 alt=3D""=20
src=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify-clip.png" =

width=3D15></A></DIV>Load capacity chart</DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>=E2=80=8E</P>
<P>Forklift trucks are available in many variations and load capacities. =
In a=20
typical warehouse setting most forklifts used have load capacities =
between one=20
to five tons. Larger machines, up to 50 tons lift capacity are used for =
lifting=20
heavier loads, including loaded <A class=3Dmw-redirect title=3D"Shipping =
containers"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shipping_containers">shipping =
containers</A>.=20
<SUP class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-6><A title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-6">[7]</A></SUP><=
/P>
<P>In addition to a control to raise and lower the forks (also known as =
blades=20
or tines), the operator can tilt the mast to compensate for a load's =
tendency to=20
angle the blades toward the ground and risk slipping off the forks. Tilt =
also=20
provides a limited ability to operate on non-level ground. Skilled =
forklift=20
operators annually compete in obstacle and timed challenges at regional =
<A=20
class=3D"external text"=20
title=3Dhttp://www.google.com/search?q=3Dforklift+rodeo&amp;start=3D0&amp=
;ie=3Dutf-8&amp;oe=3Dutf-8&amp;client=3Dfirefox-a&amp;rls=3Dorg.mozilla:e=
n-US:official=20
href=3D"http://www.google.com/search?q=3Dforklift+rodeo&amp;start=3D0&amp=
;ie=3Dutf-8&amp;oe=3Dutf-8&amp;client=3Dfirefox-a&amp;rls=3Dorg.mozilla:e=
n-US:official"=20
rel=3Dnofollow>forklift rodeos</A>.</P>
<P><A id=3DGeneral_operations name=3DGeneral_operations></A></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A title=3D"Edit section: General =
operations"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D7">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>General operations</SPAN></H3>
<DIV class=3D"thumb tright">
<DIV class=3Dthumbinner style=3D"WIDTH: 252px"><A class=3Dimage=20
title=3D"A forklift driver in training"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:PeopleForklift.jpg"><IMG=20
class=3Dthumbimage height=3D170 alt=3D""=20
src=3D"http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/PeopleFor=
klift.jpg/250px-PeopleForklift.jpg"=20
width=3D250 border=3D0></A>=20
<DIV class=3Dthumbcaption>
<DIV class=3Dmagnify><A class=3Dinternal title=3DEnlarge=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image:PeopleForklift.jpg"><IMG =
height=3D11=20
alt=3D"" =
src=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/skins-1.5/common/images/magnify-clip.png" =

width=3D15></A></DIV>A forklift driver in training</DIV></DIV></DIV>
<P>Forklifts are rated for loads at a specified maximum weight and a =
specified=20
forward <A title=3D"Center of mass"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Center_of_mass">centre of =
gravity</A>. This=20
information is located on a nameplate provided by the manufacturer, and =
loads=20
must not exceed these specifications(In Canada it is 24" Load Center). =
In many=20
jurisdictions it is illegal to remove or tamper with the nameplate =
without the=20
permission of the forklift manufacturer.</P>
<P>An important aspect of forklift operation is that many have =
rear-wheel=20
steering. While this increases maneuverability in tight cornering =
situations, it=20
differs from a driver=E2=80=99s traditional experience with other =
wheeled vehicles.=20
While steering, as there is no <A title=3D"Caster angle"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caster_angle">caster</A> action, it =
is=20
unnecessary to apply steering force to maintain a constant rate of =
turn.</P>
<P>Another critical characteristic of the forklift is its instability. =
The=20
forklift and load must be considered a unit with a continually varying =
centre of=20
gravity with every movement of the load. A forklift must never negotiate =
a turn=20
at speed with a raised load, where <A class=3Dmw-redirect=20
title=3D"Centrifugal force"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centrifugal_force">centrifugal</A> =
and <A=20
class=3Dmw-redirect title=3DGravity=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity">gravitational</A> forces =
may combine=20
to cause a disastrous tip-over accident. The forklift are designed with =
a load=20
limit for the forks which is decreased with fork elevation and =
undercutting of=20
the load (i.e. load does not butt against the fork "L"). A loading plate =
for=20
loading reference is usually located on the forklift. A forklift should =
not be=20
used as a personnel lift without the fitting of specific safety =
equipment, such=20
as a "cherry picker" or "cage".</P>
<P><A id=3DLift_truck_associations_and_organizations=20
name=3DLift_truck_associations_and_organizations></A></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Lift truck associations and organizations"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D8">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Lift truck associations and =
organizations</SPAN></H2>
<P>There are many national as well as continental associations related =
to the=20
industrial truck industry. Some of the major organizations are listed =
as:</P>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Industrial Truck Association (ITA)</B> (North America)<SUP=20
  class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-7><A title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-7">[8]</A></SUP> =

</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Material Handling Equipment Distributors Association =
(MHEDA)</B> (North=20
  America)<SUP class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-8><A title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-8">[9]</A></SUP> =

</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>F=C3=A9d=C3=A9ration Europ=C3=A9enne de la Manutention - =
European Federation of=20
  Materials Handling (FEM)</B><SUP class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-9><A =
title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-9">[10]</A></SUP>=
=20
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>British Industrial Truck Association (BITA)</B><SUP =
class=3Dreference=20
  id=3Dcite_ref-10><A title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-10">[11]</A></SUP=
>=20
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Japan Industrial Vehicles Association (JIVA)</B><SUP =
class=3Dreference=20
  id=3Dcite_ref-11><A title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-11">[12]</A></SUP=
>=20
</LI></UL>
<UL>
  <LI><B>Korean Construction Equipment Manufacturers Association=20
  (KOCEMA)</B><SUP class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-12><A title=3D""=20
  =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-12">[13]</A></SUP=
>=20
</LI></UL>
<P>There are many significant contacts among these organizations and =
they have=20
established joint statistical and engineering programs. One program is =
the=20
<B>World Industrial Trucks Statistics (WITS)</B> which is published =
every month=20
to the association memberships. The statistics are separated by area (<A =

title=3DContinent =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continent">continent</A>),=20
country and class of machine. While the statistics are generic, and do =
not count=20
production from most of the smaller manufacturers, the information is=20
significant for its depth. These contacts have brought to a common =
definition of=20
a Class System which all the major manufacturers adhere to.</P>
<P><A id=3DForklift_safety_organizations=20
name=3DForklift_safety_organizations></A></P>
<H2><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Forklift safety organizations"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D9">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Forklift safety organizations</SPAN></H2>
<P><A id=3DStandards name=3DStandards></A></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A title=3D"Edit section: Standards"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D10">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Standards</SPAN></H3>
<P>Forklift safety is subject to a variety of standards world wide. The =
most=20
important standard is the ANSI B56=E2=80=94of which stewardship has now =
been passed from=20
the <A title=3D"American National Standards Institute"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/American_National_Standards_Institut=
e">American=20
National Standards Institute</A> (ANSI) to the Industrial Truck =
Standards=20
Development Foundation after multi-year negotiations. ITSDF is a =
non-profit=20
organization whose only purpose is the promulgation and modernization of =
the B56=20
standard.<SUP class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-13><A title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-13">[14]</A></SUP=
></P>
<P>Other standards have been implemented in the United States by the <A=20
title=3D"Occupational Safety and Health Administration"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupational_Safety_and_Health_Admin=
istration">Occupational=20
Safety and Health Administration</A> (OSHA) and in the <A =
title=3D"United Kingdom"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom">United Kingdom</A> =
by the <A=20
title=3D"Health and Safety Executive"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_and_Safety_Executive">Health =
and=20
Safety Executive</A>.<SUP class=3Dreference id=3Dcite_ref-14><A =
title=3D""=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift#cite_note-14">[15]</A></SUP=
> In many=20
countries forklift truck operators must be trained and certified to =
operate=20
forklift trucks. Certification may be required for each individual class =
of lift=20
that an operator would use.</P>
<P><A id=3DForklift_Training_in_the_United_Kingdom=20
name=3DForklift_Training_in_the_United_Kingdom></A></P>
<H3><SPAN class=3Deditsection>[<A=20
title=3D"Edit section: Forklift Training in the United Kingdom"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_truck&amp;ac=
tion=3Dedit&amp;section=3D11">edit</A>]</SPAN>=20
<SPAN class=3Dmw-headline>Forklift Training in the United =
Kingdom</SPAN></H3>
<P>In the UK, <A class=3Dnew title=3D"Forklift Training (page does not =
exist)"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_Training&amp=
;action=3Dedit&amp;redlink=3D1">Forklift=20
Training</A> is carried out by a number of different organisations, =
which all=20
Forklift Instructors must be registered with at least one of them. =
Although=20
R.T.I.T.B. insist on 2 yearly refresher training, the amount of time =
determined=20
between refresher courses is subject to the H&amp;S Executive, Insurance =

companies or company policies. The H&amp;S Executive does recommend=20
re-training/testing every 3 years.</P>
<P>United Kingdom Forklift Instructors must be registered to one of the=20
following;<BR>Road Transport Industry Training Board LTD =
(RTITB)<BR>Independent=20
Training Standards Scheme and Register (ITSSAR) which the training arm =
of the=20
Association of Industrial Truck Trainers (AITT)<BR>National Plant =
Operators=20
Registration Scheme (NPORS)<BR>CITB-ConstructionSkills<BR>Lantra - =
Sector Skills=20
Council for the environmental and land-based sector<BR></P>
<P>There are various different training companies across the UK that can =
provide=20
training on-site and off-site, these can be independent instructors or =
part of a=20
training company. There are also various training centre's across the <A =

title=3D"United Kingdom" =
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom">United=20
Kingdom</A> that can provide individuals not already trained to use a =
Forklift=20
Truck to help gain a certificate of competance.</P>
<P>In the <A title=3D"United Kingdom"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom">United Kingdom</A> =
training=20
falls into four different categories;<BR>REFRESHER - People who have =
gained a=20
Forklift Training Certificate and need to be brought up to date with new =
laws=20
and/or regulations.<BR>CONVERSION - People who have been trained on a =
type of=20
truck recently, and need to start using a different =
type.<BR>SEMI-EXPERIENCED -=20
People who are competent on a <A class=3Dmw-redirect title=3D"Forklift =
Truck"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forklift_Truck">Forklift Truck</A>, =
but have=20
never been certificated.<BR>NOVICE - Never been on a Forklift Truck =
before and=20
never been certificated.<BR>The courses can last for 1 day for a =
Refresher or a=20
Conversion course, to 5 days for a Novice course. <A title=3D"United =
Kingdom"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom">United Kingdom</A> =
<A=20
class=3Dnew title=3D"Forklift Instructors (page does not exist)"=20
href=3D"http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=3DForklift_Instructors&=
amp;action=3Dedit&amp;redlink=3D1">Forklift=20
Instructors</A> are allowed to train a maximum of Three People per day, =
this=20
does not include classroom work.</P>
